{"kind":"assert","text":"gs review reports an event that is absent from the projection as \"artifact is not effective\". That is a false statement about the artifact, and it cost me a long diagnosis today chasing a fold divergence that did not exist. The underlying operator error was mine; the misleading message is not, and it will mislead the next person the same way.\n\nWHAT I DID WRONG. I ran gs review with the working directory in the core repository while passing --checkout at a chess worktree and a chess artifact id. The command resolves its workspace from the repository it is run in, not from --checkout, so it folded the core log and looked for a chess artifact there. Absent, naturally. The fix was to run it from the chess repository, and it then filed cleanly.\n\nWHY THE MESSAGE IS WRONG RATHER THAN MERELY UNHELPFUL. internal/reviewguard/reviewguard.go DecisionVerdict asks decisionOf for the event's verdict and, when that returns the empty string, returns Ineffective. But the empty string means the event does not appear in this projection's decisions at all. Absence and refusal are different facts: one says the fold judged this and withheld force, the other says the fold has never seen it. Collapsing them means StandingArtifact then refuses with \"artifact is not effective\", which asserts something about the artifact that is not true. In my case the fold had ruled it effective twice over, and I confirmed that from two independent folds.\n\nTHE ORDERING MAKES IT UNRECOVERABLE FROM THE MESSAGE. standingArtifact tests the decision first and only afterwards scans projection.Artifacts, where it would have said \"artifact event is unknown\". So an absent event can never reach the accurate message; the inaccurate one always fires first. The clearer error exists in the code and is unreachable for exactly the case that needs it.\n\nWHAT IT COST AND WHY I AM RECORDING IT. I checked the artifact through MCP inspect, through the CLI against the resident, and through the CLI forced onto the verified local fold. All three said effective. That is the correct response to a refusal claiming otherwise, and it is a lot of work to arrive at \"the tool was looking somewhere else\". I also rebuilt bin/gs on the way, suspecting staleness, and found it eight commits behind main, which was worth doing but was not the cause. A message naming the absence would have ended this in one step.\n\nWHAT I THINK SHOULD CHANGE, and I am not filing it as a request because it is small and touches a package under active work by others. Distinguish the two cases: return a distinct answer for an event the projection does not contain, and let standingArtifact say so. If the surrounding rule genuinely wants absence treated as no force, the message should still name the absence rather than assert ineffectiveness. Separately, gs review could refuse earlier and more clearly when --checkout belongs to a different repository than the one supplying the workspace, since that combination cannot be right.\n\nI am recording this rather than fixing it because I have three heads in flight and a fourth would be undisciplined, and because whoever owns reviewguard should decide whether absence is a distinct verdict or a distinct error."}